fs and stories  some legends say the mexicans began a  pilgrimage from atlan or northern  location until they were urged to leave  by witsa lapachli the god of war and  sacrifice he bid them to leave their  homeland and travel until they came to a  place where an eagle devoured a snake  resting on a cactus for more than 200  years they migrated south through land  much harsher than the home they left  behind  weeds scratched them as they walked and  rocks bit them as they slept but we  wheatsulapochley's guidance eventually  led them to a small island in the middle  of lake tex cocoa in the onawak valley  around 1325  this and most of the other stories we  know from nawa mythology come from  pictographic records that describe their  history sacred rituals and religious  beliefs the talatoani or chief speaker  of the empire was set to own a whole  library of these codices before most  were destroyed during the conquest  luckily a few were kept safe and the  surviving nawa continued to record their  history in more codices for generations  afterwards  these codices also tell us that the  universe in nagua mythology is cyclical  so that the end of the world is really  just another beginning in the true  beginning there was nothing but an  endless void from which the dual-natured  god omateodl formed you may remember  from our gender fluidity episode that  omatiotl was both male and female made  of the god oma takutly and the goddess  omasi waddle there are several examples  of similar male female pairs in nawa  mythology in this binary form omateodle  produced four sons witsila pashtly  quetzaquadol tezcatlipoca and shipe  totec each of them was associated with a  specific color bird and cardinal  direction  as omatiotl retreated to the highest  plane of heaven these four gods created  many others including the rain god  tlalak  he and witsalapashli were the most  venerated among the mashika and the  templo mayor in tenochtitlan was  dedicated to them  together the gods created and ruled the  universe which was split into three  vertical regions  in the middle was the earth made from  the body of the crocodile sea monster c  pactly  thirteen levels of heaven stretched  above sea pactly's head and nine levels  of the underworld stretched below  the earth was dark so the gods created a  sun to keep it warm and bright but  making something as powerful as the sun  is no easy feat even for a god it  requires sacrifice which is a central  theme in november mythology  tetzkatvi poka a god of night who was  adopted by the nawat from the indigenous  toltec people became the son of the  first creation but was a weak nocturnal  sun and was overthrown by his brother  quetzalcoatl a god of wind life and  light  thus started the next cycle of the nawat  universe the one where the first humans  were made but the humans became corrupt  and quetzalcoatl destroyed them with  terrifying hurricanes the few humans who  survive escaped to the tops of trees and  became monkeys  the next sun or cycle of the universe  was ruled by tlalak the god of rain and  thunder it was a prosperous time until  it ended in a barrage of fiery rain in  some legends tolok sent the fire himself  after tezcatlipoca stole his wife away  in others it was the ongoing feud  between quetzalcoatl and tezcatlipoca  that ended the third sun  the fourth god to sacrifice themself was  chao chiyu tlikue twalak's sister and  the goddess of running water the feuding  brothers struck her down and the world  ended in a great flood that collapsed  the sky and all the people were turned  into fish  in some stories tezcatlipoca taunts her  until she cries so many tears that she  floods the world  when it came time to create the fifth  sun most of the gods were understandably  hesitant to sacrifice themselves it was  nana huwatsen the humble pustulous one  who jumped into the fire first in doing  so he created tona tiyu who in some  stories ruled over the fifth son  not to be outdone tetset zakat jumped in  next and became the moon to nana  watson's son  so that they didn't repeat their  previous mistakes the other gods  sacrificed their blood to nonhawatson to  keep him strong  the world under the fifth son needed  people so quetzalcoatl descended to  mikdlon the underworld to retrieve the  bones of the first humans miktlon  takutly the lord of this land of the  dead told quetzalcoatl that he could  have the bones only if he traveled  through all of myktlan four times while  blowing a conch shell but the shell had  no holes in it it goes to show that no  matter the culture you can't expect to  get anything from a god without first  completing some impossible task  clever quetzalcoatl called the worms to  two holes in the shell and filled it  with bees so that their buzzing would  make the horns sing  when quetzalcoatl carried the bones up  to earth he ground them up and mixed his  blood with the powder to make a paste  that was used like play-doh to make  people  the nawa believed that they were some of  these humans crafted for the fifth son  and that this world would one day end in  a devastating earthquake ritual  sacrifice of humans animals and food was  central to nawat culture so important  that priests supposedly sacrificed 20  000 people in one day to honor the  completion of the great temple and  tenochtitlan  some historians say all the sacrifices  were meant to instill fear and obedience  in the nahuwat city-states that the  mexica conquered  but according to mythology from the  codices the sacrifices were meant to  empower and impeach the gods  following the story of nanahuatan's  sacrifice according to mexico state  ideology even the sun required the power  that blood provided to rise each day in  now a culture like in norse the quality  of your afterlife depended not on how  morally you lived but on the manner of  your death those who died of sacrifices  to the gods got the honor of  accompanying the sun as it rose to its  midpoint in the sky along with warriors  who died in battle  the souls of women who died in  childbirth were honored to accompany the  sun as it set to the west where it  descended to mcmahon to make its journey  back to the east  after four years of this service the  souls returned to earth as exotic birds  a nod to the god wheatsel lapachli whose  name means hummingbird of the south  people who died from drowning or  lightning strikes spent their afterlifes  in tallalucan a paradise in the lowest  level of heaven ruled by talaq and his  sister consort child cheat with liku  everyone else spent their afterlife  traveling through the nine levels of  mclean for four years the dead faced  obstacle after obstacle they climbed an  obsidian mountain braved winds so cold  they cut the skin and evaded ferocious  beasts who feasted on human hearts  eventually they reached the ninth level  the home of the lord and lady of death  where they could finally rest but they  didn't have to make the trip alone the  souls of the dead were guided by a white  dog named shalottal who by the way is  very reminiscent of zero the ghostly dog  in the nightmare before christmas  the spanish destroyed so many vestiges  of nawa culture from documents to  buildings to art but they couldn't wipe  out the culture completely when mexico  gained its independence in 1821 after  300 years of spanish rule its leaders  drew inspiration from nawa culture to  establish mexico's national identity you  can see signs of this in murals around  mexico city the flag that sports the  legendary snake chomping eagle  prophesized by witsel apostle and oh  yeah the name of the country that's  derived from the name of the meshica  people the culture isn't just alive in  mexico references to nawa mythology can  be found in graphic novels video games  and other media if you know how to spot  it  nawa culture is richly complex with  stories that reflect and explain the  sometimes confusing duality of nature  beyond mythology the nava had an  interesting political structure an  impressive agricultural system and two  calendars yet most people these days  don't even know them by their real name  dr mct i know neither of us got to learn  much about nawa culture as school kids  but i'm pretty excited we get to now  yeah and i know those little weirdos  would have loved all the bloody parts  oh for sure  [Music]  you

